<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Doda district</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Doda district is an administrative district in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chenab">Chenab</a> valley in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> division of Jammu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with its headquarters at the town of Doda. Mountainous and largely rural, it includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhadarwah">Bhadarwah</a> valley. Parts of the original district were separated in 2006 to form the Kishtwar and Ramban districts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/doda-district.jpg" alt="Doda district" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arun Singh Suryavanshi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Doda_city_in_Jammu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Doda district covers 2,625 square kilometres in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> division of Indian-administered Jammu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, in the disputed Kashmir region. Its climate varies with altitude and is temperate to sub-tropical. The Ramban and Doda tehsils are warmer, while Bhagwah, Gundna and Warwan stay snow-bound for five or six months of the year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">Monsoon</a> rains fall from July to September. The district has 406 villages and 18 tehsils.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The district was once largely inhabited by a Sarazi population before settlers arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, and Kashmiri populations settled there in the 17th and 18th centuries. Its early history is poorly documented. Various groups, including Ranas, Rajas and independent chiefs, ruled it, and in 1822 Maharaja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh</a> conquered Doda, which became the winter capital of the Kishtwar state. The English traveller G. T. Vigne visited in 1829 and described the rope bridges over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chenab">Chenab</a>. In 1846 Doda and Kishtwar became part of the new state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and Kashmir after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a> pact.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhadarwah">Bhaderwah</a> was a principality whose recorded history goes back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajatarangini">Rajatarangini</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhana">Kalhana</a>. It passed to Chamba in 1821 and to the Jammu Darbar in 1846. In 1948 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udhampur">Udhampur</a> district was partitioned and a Doda district was formed from the Ramban, Bhadarwah, Thathri and Kishtwar tehsils. Ramban became a separate district in 2006, as did Kishtwar. In the 1990s and 2000s the district saw militant violence, including massacres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> villagers in 1998, 2001 and 2006. On 13 June 2023 an earthquake of magnitude 5.0 centred near Thathri damaged buildings in Bhaderwah and Kishtwar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> gave the district 409,936 people. Muslims made up 53.82 percent and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> 45.77 percent. The Muslims are mostly ethnic Kashmiris, and a study in 2014 found about 40 percent of the population speaking Kashmiri. Pahadi languages such as Bhadarwahi and Sarazi form the second largest group, and others speak Gojri and Dogri. The town of Doda is the headquarters. The district has three assembly constituencies and is part of the Udhampur Lok Sabha constituency.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Doda district” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
